1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,967
1 a.m. A man stares at a single number.

2
00:00:03,967 --> 00:00:06,467
Bro, how long have you been staring at that "1"?

3
00:00:06,467 --> 00:00:08,567
Three hours, twelve minutes. This is research.

4
00:00:08,567 --> 00:00:12,733
Today: being left on read. What does your brain do with silence?

5
00:00:12,733 --> 00:00:17,033
One. Eight minutes of ignored texts shook people's basic needs.

6
00:00:17,033 --> 00:00:19,633
Eight minutes? I can't last eight seconds.

7
00:00:19,633 --> 00:00:22,500
They could've blamed a glitch. It didn't help.

8
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:27,700
Two. A brain scan showed activity tracking distress when players were left out of a ball game.

9
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:30,967
But exactly where? Scientists are still arguing.

10
00:00:30,967 --> 00:00:32,867
Even the brain hasn't sorted it out.

11
00:00:32,867 --> 00:00:35,700
Three. When the "read" mark shows, anxiety kicks in sooner.

12
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:38,967
Unread is bearable. Read and silent is scary.

13
00:00:38,967 --> 00:00:42,367
Four. Read receipts were linked to checking the phone more often.

14
00:00:42,367 --> 00:00:44,067
Bro, how many times just now?

15
00:00:44,067 --> 00:00:45,500
Don't talk to me. I'm counting.

16
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:48,700
Conclusion: faced with silence, the brain translates it as "my fault."

17
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:52,433
But we rarely know their side. That's just my opinion.

18
00:00:52,433 --> 00:00:56,867
Uncle's message to a limited-edition figure seller: "Is it in stock?"

19
00:00:56,867 --> 00:00:57,667
(gulp) Still a "1"…

20
00:00:57,667 --> 00:00:59,500
Three hours later. Ding.

21
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:00,433
"Sold out."

22
00:01:00,433 --> 00:01:02,067
…I preferred being left on read.
